---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatJson from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatJson.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="json-unescape">
  <DevFormatJson client:load mode="unescape" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Turning an escaped string back into text</h2>
    <p>
      Paste an escaped JSON string and JSON unescape returns the characters it stands for:
      <code>\"</code> becomes a quote, <code>\n</code> becomes a real line break,
      <code>\t</code> becomes a tab, and <code>\uXXXX</code> becomes the character at that
      codepoint. The surrounding double quotes are optional. If you leave them off, the tool adds
      them before parsing.
    </p>

    <h2>The three errors you will actually hit</h2>
    <h3>A lone backslash</h3>
    <p>
      In JSON, a backslash always starts an escape sequence. A raw Windows path such as
      <code>C:\Users\me</code> reads as &ldquo;escape U, escape m&rdquo;, and neither exists. To fix
      it, double each backslash in the source that made the string. This is the most common cause of
      an escaped string that does not parse.
    </p>
    <h3>An unterminated string</h3>
    <p>
      This is usually an incomplete copy and paste, or an unescaped quote inside the string. The parser
      then ends the string early and finds invalid text after it. Make sure there are exactly two
      unescaped quotes.
    </p>
    <h3>A short \u escape</h3>
    <p>
      <code>\u</code> must be followed by exactly four hexadecimal digits. <code>\u41</code> is
      invalid, although 41 is a valid hex byte. Write <code>\u0041</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Double-escaped text</h2>
    <p>
      If one unescape gives text that still contains <code>\"</code> and <code>\n</code>, the value was
      escaped twice. This happens when a client serialises an object to a string and a framework then
      serialises the whole request again, or when a log line records an already-serialised payload.
      Unescape a second time to read it. Then find the layer that escapes without need, because some
      other layer will later unescape the data twice when it should not.
    </p>

    <h2>Characters outside the Basic Multilingual Plane</h2>
    <p>
      JSON escapes characters above U+FFFF as a surrogate pair: two <code>\u</code> escapes that
      combine into one character. An emoji such as 🎉 is <code>\ud83c\udf89</code>. The tool joins the
      pair correctly, so emoji, rare CJK ideographs and mathematical alphanumerics come back whole, not
      as two replacement characters.
    </p>

    <h2>What to do with the result</h2>
    <p>
      If the unescaped text is a JSON document (usual when you take a payload from a log), send it to
      the <a href="/json-validator">JSON validator</a> to format and check it. If it is plain text with
      newlines, you can read or save it. In both cases, escape it again before you write it back into
      a JSON field. Unescaped text is not safe to paste into a JSON document.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
